Think it through but immediate response that comes to mind is a min-heap where array[0] aka root of the heap is the least element.

Stack can be implemented using an array and pop always returns array[0]. So there you have it.

This also means that accesses are expensive since they will cause readding the element in the right place and thats a lg(n) operation.

In order to avoid this use bounds and have pointers to bounds from the heap. So unless your access causes frequency of the accessed element
to get past the bound there is no need to reposition it within the heap.

